   in China's Grand Canal:A county-level study},
Journal = {ECOLOGICAL INDICATORS},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {189},
Month = {AUG},
Abstract = {As a large-scale linear heritage integrating heritage, economic,
   cultural, historical, and ecological dimensions, the Grand Canal's
   eco-efficiency (EE) holds substantial practical value for advancing the
   sustainable development of heritage spaces. Based on desirable output
   theory, this study evaluated the EE of 150 county-level administrative
   units within the Grand Canal core area from 2012 to 2023, systematically
   analyzing the key factors influencing EE and their spatiotemporal
   heterogeneity by employing the XGBoost-SHAP and the GTWR. The main
   findings are as follows: (1) EE exhibits a notable ``polarization{''}
   trend, with an increase in the number of both high-value (EE > 0.8) and
   low-value (EE < 0.3) areas and a decline in medium-efficiency zones.
   High-value areas are predominantly concentrated in the southern section
   of the canal. (2) EE demonstrates spatial autocorrelation, with the
   Moran's I index showing a ``W{''}-shaped fluctuation, indicating clear
   club convergence characterised by ``high-high{''} and ``low-low{''}
   agglomerations. (3) Regional disparity analysis reveals the following
   ranking of Gini coefficients across provinces and municipalities:
   Shandong > Hebei > Anhui > Jiangsu > Henan > Zhejiang > Tianjin >
   Beijing. Interregional differences have become the primary source of
   overall disparity. (4) EE is nonlinearly driven by multiple factors.
   Population size, vegetation coverage, and urbanization rate exert
   positive effects, whereas consumption level, urban-canal relationship,
   industrial structure, and government regulation show negative impacts,
   with significant spatial heterogeneity observed in the influence of each
   factor. This study provides a scientific basis for promoting ecological
   coordinated governance and differentiated policy design along the Grand
   Canal. Accordingly, this study advocates a differentiated development
   strategy tailored to regional EE levels along the Grand Canal.
   Specifically, high-efficiency regions should leverage their advantages
   to foster high-value-added green industries, such as cultural creativity
   and ecotourism. Transitional zones require bolstered technological
   innovation and policy incentives to propel them toward higher efficiency
   tiers. Low-efficiency areas must prioritize ecological restoration and
   stringent pollution control. Furthermore, establishing a cross-regional
   big data platform is recommended to facilitate collaborative governance.},
